<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">O'Donovan family</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The O'Donovans, in Irish Ó Donnabháin, are an Irish family descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donnuban-mac-cathail">Donnubán mac Cathail</a>, a 10th-century king of the Uí Fidgenti in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-limerick">County Limerick</a>. Displaced by Anglo-Norman settlers around the early 13th century, they moved south into Carbery in west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a>. There they held lordships around Castle Donovan and near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skibbereen">Skibbereen</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maccarthy">MacCarthy</a> princes until the confiscations of the 17th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odonovan-family.jpg" alt="O'Donovan family" width="320" height="354" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: self-created · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:O%27Donovan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donnuban-mac-cathail">Donnubán mac Cathail</a> became king of the Uí Fidgenti in 962, and his realm stretched along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-maigue">River Maigue</a> to the Shannon, taking in Adare, Croom and Bruree. He married a daughter of his Norse ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivar-of-limerick">Ivar of Limerick</a>, and the family intermarried heavily with the Norse; Scandinavian names such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imar">Ímar</a> stayed popular among them until the later 16th century. The Uí Fidgenti, kin of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eoganachta">Eóganachta</a>, paid no tribute to the kings of Cashel, and from 962 to the death of Amlaíb Ua Donnubáin in 1201 the family were a semi-independent ruling house within Munster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Driven from their lands along the Maigue between the late 12th and mid-13th centuries, some O'Donovans took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kerry">County Kerry</a> in 1169, while others remained near Bruree and Croom. The move of a branch south into Carbery came mainly in the mid to late 13th century, through their association with the MacCarthy Reagh; in 1260 they raided Norman lands with Fíngin Reanna Róin Mac Carthaig. Records are scarce for the next two centuries. In 1560 Donal of the Hides took the chiefship at Rosscarbery by killing his rival Diarmaid an Bhairc at his inauguration, a story whose truth has been doubted.</p><p>At their height in Carbery, between the late 16th century and the wars that followed the rising of 1641, the O'Donovans held about 100,000 acres, of which only about 15,000 were usable as farmland. After the Cromwellian confiscations Charles II granted most of it to Cromwell's soldiers in lieu of pay, and the family regained less than a twentieth. In 1878 its branches still held 17,213 acres of estates in southern Ireland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In Carbery the leading branches were Clancahill and Clan Loughlin, who between them held Glandore harbour: Clan Loughlin from Glandore Castle, a Norman castle taken from the Barretts, and Clancahill from Castle Donovan near Drimoleague, its principal seat until the early 17th century. The Carbery chiefs received the White Wand from the MacCarthy Reagh and paid him a small rent, while the chiefs of Bruree and the lands north of Kilmallock were inaugurated by their own Fidgheinte kin. Much of their income came from the harbours and bays they controlled. A northern sept near Doneraile was counted in the mid-16th-century Book of Howth among the 64 leading Gaelic families of Ireland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “O'Donovan family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Donnubán's descendants, the <b>O'Donovans</b>, moved south into Cork, where they held lordships near Skibbereen for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/state/ui-fidgenti">Ui Fidgenti</a></li></ul></section>
